        "message": "Here are three practical steps you can take right now: 1. **Gather clear documentation of your work.** Before having any conversations, pull together edit histories, emails, task tickets, or drafts that clearly show what you specifically contributed and when. 2. **Choose your conversation route:** * **Talk to the coworker directly:** If it might have been an honest oversight, you can give them a chance to correct it (for example: *\"I noticed the update went out without mentioning the parts I handled on X and Y. Could you make sure I'm credited in the next recap?\"*). * **Talk to your manager:** If it seemed deliberate, or if your coworker has a pattern of doing this, schedule a brief 1-on-1 with your manager. Focus on the work you delivered and your documentation rather than making it an attack on the coworker. 3. **Step into visible ownership on follow-ups.** If the project involves upcoming meetings, rollout phases, or stakeholder questions, actively present and speak to the parts you built so your involvement is clear to the wider team. You can pick the approach that best fits how your workplace handles conflict and how deliberate you think the omission was.",
